{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# J5 — A Dockerized Jira harness as the Data Center verification oracle\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nThere is no way to buy a Jira Data Center license to test against: Atlassian ended new DC purchases on\n30 March 2026 and no longer issues DC trial licenses. There is also **no official OpenAPI/Swagger spec for Jira\nDC** — Atlassian publishes only WADL, and the long-standing requests for one (JRASERVER-65954, JRASERVER-68539)\nremain open — so spec-driven contract testing is unavailable. Cloud's v3 swagger is not a substitute: different\nauth, ADF vs wiki markup, accountId vs name. Without an oracle, a DC adapter is unverifiable except against\nfakes that encode our own assumptions.\n\n## Approach\n\nUse the **purpose-built test image the pycontribs/jira project uses for exactly this problem** — a thin wrapper\nover `addono/jira-software-standalone` that stands up a real Jira 8.17.1 at `http://localhost:2990/jira` with\ndefault credentials `admin`/`admin`. It is unaffected by the DC trial shutdown, and it is the substrate the most\nwidely used Python Jira client validates itself against. Jira 8.17.1 is **≥ 8.14**, the release that introduced\nPersonal Access Tokens, so the PAT bearer auth the DC transport uses is exercised for real.\n\n**The image must be BUILT, not pulled — corrected against the registry, not assumed.** An earlier draft of this\nplan said to `docker run pycontribs/jira-test-image:8.17.1` and called it \"a pinned, pullable image\". That is\nfalse and was verified false before this plan was rewritten:\n\n- `docker pull pycontribs/jira-test-image:8.17.1` → `pull access denied ... repository does not exist`.\n- The Docker Hub API for `pycontribs/jira-test-image` returns `{\"message\": \"object not found\"}`. The\n  `pycontribs` Hub namespace contains exactly seven repositories, all OS base images (centos, fedora, ubuntu,\n  debian, alpine, opensuse, voidlinux). There is no `jira-test-image`.\n- That tag is a **local build name**. `pycontribs/jira` carries `docker/jira-test-image/{Dockerfile,README.md}`,\n  and the upstream README's own instruction is\n  `docker build -t pycontribs/jira-test-image:8.17.1 docker/jira-test-image`. It is published to no registry.\n- Its base, `addono/jira-software-standalone`, **is** pullable, and the exact digest pycontribs pins\n  (`sha256:04326628dc4ac36b2bfc1d0f2ebe5ba3807c1ec9cf9b18307d3c2ad7222537e9`) resolves.\n\nFeasibility was proven before this rewrite: the upstream Dockerfile was fetched and built on the target host\n(879 MB image, container starts and stays up). So the oracle is real; only the acquisition mechanism changes.\n\n**Decision: vendor a ~15-line Dockerfile in `tests/live_jira_dc/`** that `FROM`s the addono base **by digest**\nand reproduces the two things the upstream wrapper adds, with attribution to pycontribs/jira in a comment.\nRationale: building from a third-party repo at test time would make the harness depend on another project's\ndirectory layout and default branch staying put; vendoring the few lines is self-documenting and pins our own\nsupply chain. The alternative — a `git clone` of pycontribs/jira in the fixture — is rejected for that reason.\n\n**Three details are load-bearing and must survive into the vendored file as comments**, because each looks like\nremovable noise and each breaks the harness if removed:\n\n1. **`-DskipAllPrompts=true` on the `atlas-run` ENTRYPOINT is MANDATORY.** Atlassian shut down the Marketplace\n   v1 REST endpoint that AMPS hardcodes for its SDK update check, so without this flag **every cold container\n   start** fails with `FileNotFoundException` on `https://marketplace.atlassian.com/rest/1.0/plugins/...`. The\n   failure looks like a transient network fault, so a maintainer \"tidying\" the flag away would produce a\n   confusing, seemingly-flaky breakage.\n2. **`-dit` (equivalently compose's `tty: true` + `stdin_open: true`) is REQUIRED** — AMPS exits without a\n   controlling TTY. A plain compose service block is not sufficient.\n3. **Only Jira 8.x is supported.** `JIRA_VERSION` is overridable across 8.x patch versions, but 9+/11+ fail\n   Maven resolution because the upstream pom is anchored to the 8.x dependency graph. The README must say so,\n   or a future reader will assume the harness can track a client's newer Jira by bumping a variable.\n\n**The image does NOT contain Jira — `atlas-run` downloads it at runtime.** Verified by running it: the\ncontainer log shows `Configured Artifact: com.atlassian.jira.plugins:jira-plugin-test-resources:8.17.1:zip`\nfollowed by ~917 `Downloading from atlassian-public: https://maven.atlassian.com/...` lines before Tomcat and\nH2 come up. Four consequences the harness must design around:\n\n- **Egress to `maven.atlassian.com` is a hard RUNTIME dependency**, not a one-time image pull. An\n  egress-restricted CI runner cannot start this harness at all.\n- **`pinning the image digest does NOT pin Jira itself`.** If Atlassian withdraws the 8.17.1 artifacts from\n  their public Maven repo, the harness stops working despite our pinned base digest. That, not the image, is\n  the real fragility, and the README must say so — it also strengthens the case for the separately-tracked\n  real-instance validation (`9895-ac7d-4276-4f08`).\n- First start is dominated by that download, so the readiness budget must cover a large artifact fetch, not\n  just JVM boot.\n- The fixture SHOULD mount a persistent Maven cache (`~/.m2`) as a volume, or every fresh container\n  re-downloads ~900 artifacts.\n\n**No licensing claim is made.** An earlier draft said the image \"carries its own license\". That is a legal\ncharacterization this story has not verified, so it is replaced by the observed behaviour: no license key is\nsupplied to build or start the instance, and the Atlassian SDK's `atlas-run` provisions a local developer\ninstance. The README states it that way.\n\n**Platform is a real variable, not a footnote.** The base image is `linux/amd64`. On an `arm64` host (Apple\nSilicon, ARM CI runners) it runs under emulation, and the upstream README's \"first responsive request within\n~3-5 minutes\" does not hold — observed startup on an emulated arm64 host is materially slower. So the compose\nfile sets `platform: linux/amd64` **explicitly** (making behaviour deterministic instead of dependent on the\nhost's default resolution), and the readiness probe's timeout is generous and overridable by env var rather\nthan a hardcoded few minutes. The README records that a native-amd64 runner is strongly preferred.\n\n**Stated limitation, deliberately accepted:** 8.17.1 is older than the version a client such as CMS is likely\nrunning, so this harness proves REST v2 protocol semantics, not deployment-specific behavior (workflow names,\ncustom fields, permission schemes, user directories). Closing that gap requires access to the real instance and\nis tracked separately as ticket `9895-ac7d-4276-4f08`; this story's README records the exact image tag so that\ncomparison has a baseline.\n\nAdd `tests/live_jira_dc/` with a compose file, a readiness probe that polls until the instance answers REST,\nand a fixture that provisions a scratch project and tears down everything it created. **Every test in the\ndirectory carries the existing `integration` marker** — not a new custom marker — because `make test` runs\n`pytest -m \"not integration and not external\"` (**`Makefile:178`** — verified; an earlier draft said 154, which is\ninside the `actionlint-bin` recipe), so the suite is excluded by construction with\nno Makefile change and no filter-expression edit.\n\nTeardown follows **`docs/adr/0037-reconciler-live-validation-harness.md` §3** (named in full because FIVE ADRs\nshare the `0037-` prefix — code-review-novelty-convergence, cross-ticket-overlap, oss-contribution-intake-posture,\nreconciler-live-validation-harness, transport-retry — so a bare \"ADR 0037\" is unresolvable), which\n**prohibits post-deletion search-index assertions** because Jira's index\nlags creates and deletes by an unbounded interval and can return stale results either way. So the teardown\noracle is not \"query the project and find it empty\": it asserts each delete call's HTTP status and then polls\neach issue's **direct REST endpoint** (`/rest/api/2/issue/{key}`) until it returns 404, which is index-independent.\n\n**Alternative rejected: fixtures and a stateful fake alone.** rebar's test-design standard allows a verified\nfake for external-service mapping, but a fake verified only against fixtures we recorded cannot falsify\nassumptions we never thought to record — transitions, permission errors, link direction. The fake is still\nbuilt (J6 uses it for fast unit tests); this story gives it something real to be verified against.\n\n**Alternative rejected: reusing pycontribs/jira's test assertions as the oracle.** Their tests assert their\nclient's behavior, not rebar's field mapping. The reusable asset is the image and harness pattern, which is\nwhat this story takes.\n\n**Alternative rejected: the Atlassian SDK's `atlas-run-standalone`.** It also yields a licensed local Jira, but\nit needs the full SDK toolchain and its licenses are short-lived (3-day timebombs requiring `atlas-mvn clean`\nrecycling), which is hostile to repeatable CI. A pinned Docker image has neither problem.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `tests/live_jira_dc/Dockerfile` (new) — the vendored wrapper: `FROM` the addono base **by digest**, the\n  `-DskipAllPrompts=true` entrypoint, and the three rationale comments above plus attribution to\n  pycontribs/jira.\n- `tests/live_jira_dc/docker-compose.yml`, `conftest.py`, `README.md` (new) — compose `build`s the local\n  Dockerfile (it does NOT reference an unpullable image), sets `platform: linux/amd64`, `tty: true`,\n  `stdin_open: true`, and publishes `2990:2990`.\n- `tests/live_jira_dc/test_harness_smoke.py` (new).\n- `Makefile` — a `jira-dc-up` target only; the `test:` target is NOT modified (the `integration` marker is\n  already excluded by its existing `-m` expression at **`Makefile:178`**), and `jira-dc-up` is added to the\n  `.PHONY` list at `Makefile:30` — without that, a file or directory named `jira-dc-up` would make the target\n  silently no-op.\n\n## Testing\n\nReal-external-service tier by construction — this story *is* the harness. Its own oracle is that the harness\nstarts, is usable, and provably cleans up.\n\n- happy path: the harness starts, the readiness probe reports REST answering, and a smoke test creates and\n  reads back one issue through raw REST v2.\n- auth path: a Personal Access Token minted on the 8.17.1 instance authenticates a request via\n  `Authorization: Bearer`, proving the DC auth mode the transport will use is exercised, not assumed.\n- teardown oracle (index-independent, per ADR 0037 §3): after a deliberately failing test, every issue the\n  fixture created returns 404 from its direct `/rest/api/2/issue/{key}` endpoint, and each delete call's HTTP\n  status was 2xx. Search is never consulted.\n- gating oracle: `pytest --collect-only -m \"not integration and not external\"` collects zero tests from\n  `tests/live_jira_dc/`.\n- failure path: with the instance down, the marked suite fails with a clear \"harness not running\" message from\n  the readiness probe rather than a raw connection traceback.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] the harness **builds from the vendored `tests/live_jira_dc/Dockerfile`** and starts (via the compose\n      file or the `jira-dc-up` target), and the readiness probe reports REST v2 answering at\n      `http://localhost:2990/jira`. The criterion is explicitly BUILD-and-start, not pull-and-start: no\n      acceptance step may depend on `pycontribs/jira-test-image` being pullable, because it is not published\n- [ ] the vendored `Dockerfile` pins the addono base **by digest** (not by tag), keeps\n      `-DskipAllPrompts=true` on the `atlas-run` entrypoint, and carries a comment explaining that removing\n      that flag breaks every cold start via the retired Marketplace v1 endpoint\n- [ ] the compose service sets `platform: linux/amd64`, `tty: true` and `stdin_open: true`, and the readiness\n      timeout is env-overridable — so an emulated arm64 host is slow rather than spuriously failing\n- [ ] a smoke test creates and reads back an issue via REST v2, and a PAT-authenticated request succeeds\n- [ ] after an intentionally failing test run, every created issue returns 404 from its direct issue endpoint\n      (no search query used anywhere in teardown verification)\n- [ ] `pytest --collect-only -m \"not integration and not external\"` reports 0 tests collected from\n      `tests/live_jira_dc/`, and `git diff Makefile` shows no change to the `test:` target\n- [ ] **every live test against the harness sets `allow_insecure = true` explicitly and asserts the cleartext\n      warning is emitted** — this is an obligation the parent epic assigns to J5 (e369 Approach step 7: the\n      harness serves plain `http://localhost:2990/jira`, so it is the INTENDED `allow_insecure` case, and\n      routing the loopback path through the override rather than around the validator is the whole point).\n      Omitting it would leave the override's only real exercise unwritten\n- [ ] the PAT is created **programmatically** by the fixture via `POST /rest/pat/latest/tokens` authenticated\n      with the admin basic credentials (the Jira DC 8.14+ endpoint), and the resulting token is then used in an\n      `Authorization: Bearer` request — so the auth AC is self-contained and does not depend on a\n      hand-minted token\n- [ ] the readiness probe has a **stated default timeout of 20 minutes** with a 5-second poll cadence,\n      overridable by env var, and on expiry it fails with an explicit \"harness not running / not ready\" message\n      naming the compose target — never a raw connection traceback. The generous default is deliberate: an\n      emulated amd64 base plus a ~900-artifact Maven download makes upstream's quoted 3-5 minutes unattainable\n      on an arm64 runner\n- [ ] `jira-dc-up` brings up a **fresh** instance (`--force-recreate`, and the fixture refuses to run against a\n      container it did not start into a known-clean state), so an interrupted previous teardown cannot leak\n      stale Jira state into the next run\n- [ ] the compose port binding is **loopback-scoped (`127.0.0.1:2990:2990`)**, not `0.0.0.0` — this instance has\n      well-known `admin`/`admin` credentials and must not be reachable from other hosts on the network\n- [ ] the `integration`-marker exclusion has a **structural backstop**, mirroring how the `external` tier is\n      already protected: a test asserts every test module under `tests/live_jira_dc/` carries the marker, so a\n      future file added without it cannot silently join the default `make test` run\n- [ ] the feasibility evidence is **recorded reproducibly** in the README — the base digest, the resolved image\n      size, the host architecture it was verified on, and the observed startup phases — rather than asserted as\n      a one-off local success\n- [ ] `tests/live_jira_dc/README.md` records the pinned base digest and Jira version, states the\n      version-drift limitation naming ticket `9895-ac7d-4276-4f08` as where real-instance validation is\n      tracked, AND records the two operational constraints a reader would otherwise rediscover the hard way:\n      that only Jira 8.x works on this base (9+/11+ fail Maven resolution), and that the base is amd64 so an\n      arm64 host emulates it and starts materially slower than the upstream's quoted 3-5 minutes"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785361436601699001,"uuid":"15f718cb-1722-4c06-ac5e-68532ea9a6d3"}